AP Log Analyzer — Consolidated Prompt Specification
2022 Subaru WRX (FA24DIT) · Dmann 91-FLEX · AccessPort CSV logs
Contents: Parts 1–6 (core rewrite) followed by Appendices A–C (Knock Control, Fueling, Boost) derived from NAMR/Atlas documentation and Cobb threshold guidance.
 Part 0 — Preamble
Major revision to AP Log Analyzer. The current rule logic produces false findings because it evaluates thresholds without regard to what the engine was actually doing. Rewrite around an operating-state model. Real-log validation has proven the following; treat it as ground truth, not suggestion.
Part 1 — Corrections to existing behaviour
•	Delimiter: real logs are comma-delimited. Detect from the header (comma, tab, semicolon) — never hardcode.
•	Encoding: degree-symbol columns (AVCS Exh Left (°), Feedback Knock (°), etc.) are not UTF-8. Read as latin-1 or replace invalid bytes. If mishandled, those column names won't match and the knock channels map to nothing — the app then reports zero knock on every log, forever. Verify Feedback Knock and Fine Knock Learn mapped successfully and hard-fail the parse if not.
•	Sample rate is variable and jittery: observed 0.003–0.102 s, median 0.025. All windowed math must use real time deltas. Never multiply sample counts by an assumed rate.
Part 2 — Operating-state classifier (new, and the core of the rewrite)
Before any rule runs, classify every sample into exactly one state. Rules subscribe to states. This replaces all raw threshold gating.
State	Condition
IDLE	RPM < 1200, throttle < 5%
DECEL_FUEL_CUT	IDC < 10% or load < 0.5 g/rev, with throttle < 25%
CRUISE	boost < 0, throttle 5–60%, steady RPM
TIP_IN	throttle rising > 200%/sec, or boost rising > 15 psi/sec
SPOOL	throttle > 90%, boost rising, not yet within 2 psi of target
WOT_STEADY	throttle > 90%, boost > 4 psi, boost within 2 psi of target, sustained ≥ 0.3 s
SHIFT	throttle drops > 50% within 0.2 s while RPM > 3000
OVERRUN	throttle < 10%, RPM falling, boost < −5 psi

A WOT pull = contiguous SPOOL → WOT_STEADY, minimum 1.0 s in WOT_STEADY, RPM rising monotonically ≥ 800 RPM. Report gear, RPM span, peak boost, peak load per pull.
Validated against real data: logs containing only brief throttle stabs (8 samples > 90% throttle, 0.2 s, peak boost 0.4 psi) must produce zero pulls. If your detector finds a pull there, it is wrong.
Add a 300 ms settling exclusion after every DECEL_FUEL_CUT and SHIFT state. Wideband and fuel-trim data in that window are meaningless. This single rule eliminates the majority of observed false findings.
Part 3 — Rule rewrites
AFR
Delete the fixed 12.5:1 threshold. The tune commands ~13.0–13.2 AFR at WOT on E78 (≈ lambda 0.89), and delivers within 0.07 of commanded. A 12.5 threshold, or the common "11.5–12.0 at WOT" rule of thumb, is EJ pump-gas guidance and will condemn a correctly-fuelled flex tune.
New rule:
•	State must be WOT_STEADY
•	Compare actual vs Comm Fuel Final, never an absolute number
•	Flag only if actual is leaner than commanded by > 0.7 AFR for ≥ 5 consecutive samples
•	Never state a generic AFR target in finding text — commanded is the target
Validated: a raw Boost > 0 gate flagged AFR 15.97 as dangerous. At that sample boost was 0.18 psi, commanded was 14.70 (stoich), and it was fuel-cut recovery mid-shift. The new rule must not flag it.
Boost
•	Overshoot: state WOT_STEADY only, sustained > 2 psi over target for ≥ 0.5 s
•	Exclude samples where Target Boost Final Rel is falling faster than 5 psi/sec. The tune deliberately ramps target down at high RPM (measured: 6.98 → 3.52 psi from 5000 to 5900 RPM in 3rd) and during lift. Comparing against a collapsing target produced +11.22 psi false overshoots on every gearchange. Gated correctly, mean error across all WOT is +0.03 psi.
•	"Boost holds to redline" must be judged as tracking target, not as absolute boost staying flat.
Knock — classify, don't just threshold
Every knock event must be assigned a class before reporting:
•	LOAD_KNOCK — state SPOOL or WOT_STEADY. Real. Report with gear, RPM, boost, load, timing before and after, and DAM.
•	CRUISE_NOISE — boost < 0, throttle < 60%, load < 1.5, value exactly −1.05 (one correction step), self-recovering. Report in an informational section, not as a fault. Validated: this is the dominant pattern in normal driving, appears identically on FBK or FKL depending on whether that load/RPM cell has already learned, and correlates with mechanical/drivetrain noise rather than detonation.
Severity for LOAD_KNOCK:
•	Ugly: DAM < 1.0 at any point, or FBK ≤ −4°, or FKL ≤ −2° during a pull, or any knock that fails to recover within 2 s
•	Bad: FBK −2 to −3.9 sustained
•	Informational: single-step −1.05 that recovers with DAM held at 1.0
Tuner guidance to encode: −3 or shallower with DAM at 1.0 is not cause for concern. Present factually, do not alarm.
KS Noise Cyl 1–4: chart only. Create no rules whatsoever. Measured across real logs: clean WOT mean 934, knocking WOT mean 907, cruise knock mean 866. The channel does not discriminate. Any threshold placed on it would generate noise indefinitely.
Timing context (new — high value)
For each pull, report minimum timing vs gear, RPM and load, and chart timing against load. Measured behaviour: 3rd gear 12.5–15.5°, 4th 7.5–11.5°, 5th 3.0–7.5° at similar load. Progressive retard in taller gears is correct tune behaviour and is where knock margin is thinnest. Flag as Bad only if timing is pulled below 0° or if a LOAD_KNOCK occurs alongside timing already at its floor.
Fuel trims
•	Exclude DECEL_FUEL_CUT and OVERRUN entirely. Validated: AF Correction reads −25.8% at 1.8% IDC during coast — meaningless. Cruise-only values are −1.7% ±3.6 and +1.2% ±2.4, both healthy.
•	AF Learning 1 independently vs ±10%. Bank 2 does not exist on this car; do not synthesise it.
•	Report cruise-window mean and standard deviation, not min/max.
Fuel pressure
Apply a 0.5 s rolling median before evaluating. Raw channel is noisy: 47–59 psi at idle, 31–75 under load, with no fault present. Flag only a smoothed drop > 20% sustained ≥ 0.5 s within a pull.
Temperature
•	IAT > 120 °F during a pull only. Cruise heatsoak in traffic is normal and must not be flagged.
•	Oil > 250 °F, coolant > 220 °F.
•	Report IAT at pull start alongside every knock finding — knock at 64 °F IAT and at 120 °F mean very different things.
Ethanol
•	Use Ethanol Conc FINAL, never RAW.
•	Report per session and track the trend across stored sessions. Measured: 73.6 → 73.6 → 74.1 → 78.1, rising. Flag when three consecutive sessions exceed 75%.
Part 4 — Anti-false-positive requirements
These are the failure modes that made the previous version untrustworthy. Every one must be enforced.
•	No rule fires on a single sample. Minimum 5 consecutive qualifying samples or 0.15 s, whichever is longer.
•	No rule fires during SHIFT, TIP_IN, DECEL_FUEL_CUT, or within 300 ms after them.
•	No absolute threshold where a commanded channel exists. AFR, boost, and fuelling are all compared to commanded values.
•	If a log contains no WOT pull, all pull-dependent rules report NOT EVALUATED — no WOT pull in this log. Never "no knock detected." Validated: three of four real logs contain no pull at all; a clean bill of health on those logs is a lie.
•	If a monitor is missing, report CANNOT EVALUATE — monitor not logged. Never a pass.
•	Every finding must carry: timestamp, RPM, gear, boost, load, throttle, operating state, and the observed vs expected value. A finding without full context is not actionable.
Part 5 — Report structure
SESSION      file, duration, tune name from AP Info, ethanol, ambient IAT
DRIVE TYPE   "cruise only - no pulls"  |  "N WOT pulls detected"
PULLS        per pull: gear, RPM span, peak boost vs target, peak load,
             min timing, knock, AFR vs commanded, IAT
UGLY         actionable now, or "none"
BAD          investigate, or "none"
INFORMATIONAL  cruise-noise knock events, expected tune behaviour
GOOD         explicitly verified, each stating what was checked
NOT EVALUATED  every rule that could not run, and why

The NOT EVALUATED section is mandatory and must never be empty on a cruise-only log.
Part 6 — Regression tests
Build these as fixtures in /docs/test/ and run on every change:
•	Cruise-only log → 0 pulls, all pull rules NOT EVALUATED, cruise-noise knock listed as informational
•	Throttle stab (8 samples > 90%, 0.2 s, 0.4 psi) → 0 pulls detected
•	Fuel-cut recovery sample (AFR 15.97, boost 0.18, commanded 14.70) → no AFR finding
•	Gearshift target collapse (boost 5.61, target 2.50, throttle 46%) → no overshoot finding
•	Real load-knock (FBK −1.05, 9.4 psi, load 2.45, 100% throttle, gear 5, DAM 1.0) → classified LOAD_KNOCK, severity informational, with timing 5.0→3.5 reported
•	High-RPM target ramp-down (target 6.98→3.52 while actual holds 7) → no overshoot finding
•	Decel AF Correction −25.8% at 1.8% IDC → no fuel trim finding
•	Degree-symbol columns in latin-1 → knock channels map successfully
Each test asserts both that correct findings appear and that incorrect ones do not. The second assertion is the one that matters.
All prior constraints remain binding: static site, no build step, no keys, zero external requests, default-src 'self', system fonts, vendored MIT charting, IndexedDB, public repo with *.csv and *.icloud ignored, runs from index.html on the filesystem.
Once it's built, run log 3 first. If it reports anything in Good or Ugly rather than filling NOT EVALUATED, the state classifier isn't working — and that's the failure that made the first version untrustworthy.
 Appendices — Domain Model Blocks (append to prompt)
Appendices A–C are additive to Parts 1–6. Where an appendix and a Part conflict, the Part wins: Parts encode behaviour validated against this specific car's logs, appendices encode general platform documentation.
Appendix A — Knock Control Model (FA24 DIT)
A1. Threshold and severity layer (Cobb-derived)
KNOCK CONTROL MODEL (FA24 DIT)

Timing model: final = (dynamic_advance_max x DAM) + FBK + FKL
DAM scales an entire table. FBK/FKL are additive degrees.
Therefore DAM is the primary health metric; FBK/FKL are secondary.
All FBK/FKL values are IGNITION TIMING degrees, never fuel.

EVENT DETECTION
- One FBK event = transition to a MORE negative value.
- Monotonic return toward zero = decay of the SAME event. Do not count.
- Report: event count, peak magnitude, samples-to-recovery.

SEVERITY (load-dependent)
Low load / cruise (<1.0 g/rev, throttle <40%):
  0 to -2.5        NORMAL, likely noise
  -2.5 to -4.2     MONITOR
  worse than -4.2  INVESTIGATE
WOT / high load:
  FBK 0 to -1.4    NORMAL
  FBK -1.4 to -2.8 MONITOR (escalate if repeatable across pulls
    and not coincident with throttle tip-in)
  FBK worse than -2.8  INVESTIGATE
  FKL worse than -2.8  INVESTIGATE
  FKL -1.4 spanning a large RPM span = multiple cells = INVESTIGATE
DAM: any value below 1.000 = INVESTIGATE regardless of FBK/FKL.
  On DAM<1.0, also check boost vs target and AFR vs commanded for
  failsafe/enrichment behaviour, and report together.

SPECIAL FLAGS
- FKL present with no preceding FBK in same cell = high-intensity
  event, ECU skipped feedback stage. Escalate one level.
- FA24 DIT FKL authority is ~1 degree total. Do not apply EJ-era
  thresholds to FKL magnitude on this platform.

FALSE-KNOCK TAGGING (tag, exclude from severity count, still list)
- Clutch engagement from stop
- Gear change (gear channel transition +/-1s)
- Low RPM + high gear + partial throttle (lugging)
- Steady-state cruise, negative boost
- Abrupt throttle rate change (tip-in/tip-out)

PULL VALIDITY GATE (run BEFORE scoring any WOT pull)
Require: single gear, throttle >=95% and stable, RPM monotonic rising,
no shift, duration >=2s. If failed, output "UNSCOREABLE PULL - dirty
data" with the reason. Do NOT issue a knock verdict on a failed gate.

REPORTING RULE
Never report a raw count of negative samples. Report events, peak,
load context, and false-knock classification.
A2. Mechanism layer (NAMR/Atlas-derived)
KNOCK CONTROL MODEL - SUBARU DI (FA20/FA24) - MECHANISM LAYER

SENSORS
Two piezoelectric knock sensors, rear of crankcase, one per bank.
  LEFT  -> cylinders 3 and 1
  RIGHT -> cylinders 4 and 2
If per-bank channels exist, report bank attribution.
  Single-bank recurrence -> physical/local cause (plug, injector,
    deposits, local noise source). Recommend cylinder-level inspection.
  Both-bank -> global cause (fuel quality, IAT, timing, load).
ECU samples only within the firing cylinder's ignition window, band-
filtered. Any noise landing in that window is a detonation candidate.
Use this to explain false knock; do not call the sensor "wrong."

EVENT SHAPE CLASSIFICATION (apply before magnitude thresholds)
  Single step, no subsequent deepening within event -> NOISE-LIKE
    (docs cite ~-1.41 non-increasing as characteristic)
  Successive deepening steps within one event -> GENUINE, escalate
  Monotonic return toward 0.0 -> recovery, same event, do not count
Report trajectory shape alongside peak magnitude.

RECURRENCE IS THE DECIDING TEST
Bucket every correction by (RPM band x load g/rev cell x gear).
  Seen once, not reproducible -> LIKELY NOISE, especially if shallow
  Recurring across sessions in the same cell -> REAL FINDING:
    either true detonation or condition-specific mechanical noise
Severity = f(depth, trajectory, recurrence). Recurrence dominates.
Cross-session comparison required for any confident verdict.

FKL SEMANTICS
FKL is EEPROM-persisted per RPM x load cell.
An FKL value may be RECALLED with no live event in this log.
  Do not report recalled FKL as a new event.
Clears only per-cell, only when that cell sees no events.
Report WHICH CELLS carry learned subtraction, never "FKL cleared."

DAM ASYMMETRY
DAM falls fast on significant events; rises slowly, and only when
detonation is absent during CRITICAL/HIGH-LOAD operation.
  Therefore: DAM recovery is only meaningful if the log contains
  qualifying WOT/high-load operation. If the log is cruise-only,
  report "DAM stable, recovery not testable in this session."

PER-EVENT CONTEXT BLOCK (emit for every flagged correction)
  Load state: decel / light load / heavy load (pull) / transient
  Fuel: type and octane (incl. ethanol content if known)
  Recent mechanical changes: NEW PARTS OR MOUNTS raise block
    vibration and are a documented cause of corrections
  Recent tune changes
  Bank attribution if available
  Reproducibility: seen in N of M comparable sessions

CANDIDATE CAUSES (remediation section, ordered)
  Faulty spark plugs or fuel injectors
  AFR too lean OR too rich for cylinder conditions
  Low or degraded octane (fuel aged in tank; oil/coolant contamination)
  Ignition timing too advanced
  Effective compression raised by carbon deposits
  Excessive cylinder temperature / hot spots (carbon deposits) / high IAT
Note: carbon deposits act via TWO mechanisms (compression + hot spots)
and are especially relevant on direct-injection engines.

NARRATIVE - WHY TIMING IS PULLED
Retarding spark moves combustion later; as the piston descends,
cylinder volume rises, density/pressure/temperature fall, suppressing
detonation. Cost is torque via reduced mechanical advantage.
Use when explaining perceived power loss during corrections.
Appendix B — Fueling Model (Subaru DI, FA20/FA24)
FUELING MODEL - SUBARU DI (FA20/FA24)

WORK IN LAMBDA INTERNALLY. Lambda is fuel-independent.
Convert to AFR only for display, using LOGGED ethanol content:
  stoich AFR ~= 14.7 (E0) ... ~9.76 (E85); interpolate by ethanol %.
  NEVER hardcode 14.7. On E70-80 stoich is ~10.3-10.8:1.
Flag as ERROR any AFR interpretation made without ethanol context.

TRIM TAXONOMY - never sum, average, or interchange these
  A/F Correction #1 : wideband, trims INJECTOR PULSE WIDTH, short-term,
                      CLOSED LOOP ONLY, activation gated by RPM x load
                      ("excitation") -> interpret PER CELL, not globally
  A/F Learning #1   : wideband, trims PULSE WIDTH, long-term
  A/F Correction #3 : narrowband, trims AIR-FUEL RATIO, short-term,
                      near-stoich / idle conditions only
  A/F Learning #3   : narrowband, trims AIR-FUEL RATIO, long-term
Do not report #3 activity during pulls as meaningful.

LOOP STATE GATE - required on every AFR statistic
Emit loop state with all AFR results. Grade regimes separately.
  CLOSED LOOP (cruise/light load): feedback active. Deviation from
    target indicates trim working; assess correction magnitude,
    not raw deviation.
  OPEN LOOP (WOT/high load): NO feedback correction exists.
    Deviation from commanded = calibration/compensation issue.
    Closed-loop accuracy does NOT imply open-loop accuracy.
    Never generalise a cruise AFR result to a WOT verdict.

CELL-BASED REPORTING
Bucket AFR corrections by RPM x load (g/rev), same grid as knock.
Never report a single session-wide average as the headline.
Surface worst cell and its magnitude; a small mean can hide a
large localised error.

LEARNING #1 INTERPRETATION (flex fuel)
Correlate Learning #1 against ethanol content across sessions.
  Learning tracks ethanol changes      -> NORMAL flex adaptation
  Learning drifts, ethanol stable      -> INVESTIGATE:
      MAF calibration (esp. aftermarket intake), intake leak,
      injector performance, intake-valve carbon deposits
  Learning near its authority limit    -> ESCALATE. ECU out of range;
      closed loop corrects only within defined limits.

SENSOR LAG
Wideband feedback is delayed (transport + processing + smoothing).
When correlating AFR excursions with knock events, apply lag
compensation; do not co-timestamp naively, especially in transients.
If loop feedback delay exceeds tolerance, closed loop is unavailable.

KNOCK <-> FUELING CROSS-REFERENCE (mandatory)
For any knock finding, retrieve AFR/lambda state in the SAME
RPM x load cell. Report jointly.
  Knock + lean deviation in same cell = escalate; strongest fueling-
    related detonation signal.
  Knock + rich deviation = also a documented detonation contributor
    (incomplete combustion); do not dismiss.
  Knock with AFR on target = points away from fueling; weight
    mechanical noise, octane, IAT, deposits, timing instead.

MAF NOTE
Factory Subaru DI load calculation is MAF-based (g/rev derived from
g/s). Speed density via MAP is non-factory. If intake hardware has
changed, treat load and therefore ALL cell assignments as suspect
until Learning #1 has stabilised.
Appendix C — Boost Model (Subaru DIT, FA20DIT/FA24DIT)
BOOST MODEL - SUBARU DIT (FA20DIT/FA24DIT)

ABSOLUTE VS RELATIVE - CRITICAL
ECU targets MANIFOLD ABSOLUTE PRESSURE, not gauge boost.
  displayed_target_psi + 14.70 = ECU absolute target
  realized_boost = ECU_absolute_target - current_barometric
At non-sea-level baro, realized boost DIFFERS from displayed target.
  (e.g. 16.00 displayed -> 30.70 abs; at 12.00 baro -> 18.70 boost)
Work in ABSOLUTE internally; convert to gauge only for display,
using LOGGED barometric pressure.
If baro is absent from the log: state this, and mark all boost error
figures UNVALIDATED. Do not present a confident boost verdict.
Note: baro compensation tables usually operate in the ABSOLUTE
domain - do not assume relative-domain scaling.

PHASE SEGMENTATION - grade each phase separately
For every WOT event, segment and score independently:
  1 TIP-IN    : target steps up, error rises sharply (esp. from
                vacuum). This is NORMAL. Do not flag.
  2 SPOOL     : error closing toward zero.
                TURBO LAG = time(target increase) -> time(error = 0)
                Report per pull and per gear; track across sessions.
                Lengthening lag on same car -> boost leak / wastegate /
                actuator. INVESTIGATE.
  3 OVERSHOOT : error crosses positive -> negative. Normal if brief
                (<0.5s). Threshold: error must NOT go below -1.50 psi.
                Worse than -1.50 psi -> FLAG (mechanical damage risk).
  4 STEADY WOT: spec -1.00 to +1.00 psi. Tighter = better.
                This is the ONLY window the +/-1.00 spec applies to.

REPORTING RULE - never a single mean
Report at minimum:
  max positive error (spool)
  max negative error (overshoot)
  mean error over STEADY-STATE WOT window only
  turbo lag
A session-wide mean near zero is meaningless: positive spool error
and negative overshoot cancel. Never headline a single average.

PI CONTROLLER (FA20DIT/FA24DIT = P and I only, no D)
  P reacts immediately to error; effective transient + steady state.
  I accumulates over time; effective steady state only.
  INTEGRAL RESETS TO ZERO on closed-loop entry/exit, including
  GEAR SHIFTS.
Therefore: EXCLUDE post-shift windows from boost control evaluation.
Error immediately after a shift is expected, not a fault.
Absence of a D term is why brief overshoot is characteristic here.
If commanded wastegate position/duty is logged:
  error + wastegate at limit      -> hardware/capability constraint
  error + authority remaining     -> controller calibration issue

DTC MAPPING
  Excessive NEGATIVE error (overboost)  -> P0234, P226B
  Excessive POSITIVE error (underboost) -> P0299, P226C
Name the applicable code when reporting sustained error.

KNOCK <-> BOOST CROSS-REFERENCE (mandatory)
If a knock event falls within an OVERSHOOT window, report jointly
and ESCALATE: overshoot is a transient pressure/temperature excursion
and a documented detonation mechanism. Causally coherent pairing;
materially stronger than either finding alone.
Also cross-check boost vs target whenever DAM < 1.000 (failsafe
boost reduction is part of the DI knock strategy).
Appendix D — Precedence and conflict resolution
•	Parts 1–6 override appendices where they conflict — they are validated against this vehicle's actual logs.
•	Known conflict: Appendix A2 pull-validity gate requires throttle ≥95% and duration ≥2 s; Part 2 uses >90% and ≥1.0 s in WOT_STEADY. Use Part 2's definition for pull detection; use A2's stricter gate only to decide whether a detected pull is SCOREABLE for knock verdicts.
•	Known conflict: Appendix A1 low-load severity band (0 to −2.5 NORMAL) and Part 3 CRUISE_NOISE class both cover cruise −1.05. Part 3 wins: classify as CRUISE_NOISE, informational.
•	Appendix C absolute-pressure handling supersedes any relative-only boost math elsewhere. If barometric pressure is not a logged channel, add it; until then every boost error figure carries an UNVALIDATED marker.
